.TH WRIT\-ROTATE 1 2026-10-12 "writ 0.1.0" "writ manual"
.SH NAME
writ\-rotate \- replace the keys of an identity, keeping its id
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nh
.ad l
\fBwrit rotate\fR \-i DIR \-\-next\-key PATH... (\-\-next\-out PATH | \-\-same\-next) [\-\-key\-out PATH] [\-\-key\-pub PATH]... [\-\-key\-k K] [\-\-next\-n N] [\-\-next\-k K] [\-\-next\-pub PATH]... [\-\-max\-validity DUR | \-\-no\-max\-validity] [\-q]
.ad
.hy
.SH DESCRIPTION
Rotate the identity in DIR: replace its current keys, and keep its id.
.PP
The next keys (\-\-next\-key) sign the rotation, and nothing else. writ makes a new current key and writes it to \-\-key\-out, or in place of DIR/current.key. Then it makes new next keys and writes them to \-\-next\-out, or, with \-\-same\-next, keeps the next keys you have. Keeping them suits next keys held by friends or kept in a safe.
.PP
A rotation lists the statements that stay valid. writ keeps every statement and signature in DIR/issued.writ that is still valid: correctly signed, not revoked, not expired, and not a name or grant for a leaf key. Anything else signed with the old current key becomes invalid for every verifier that sees the rotation. Use this to recover from a stolen current key. If whoever stole it could also write to DIR/issued.writ, read it first (writ show < DIR/issued.writ) and revoke anything you did not sign.
.PP
With \-\-key\-pub, the current keys include other people's keys, and \-\-key\-k of them must sign each statement. Use this for an identity that a group shares.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIDIR\fR
the identity to rotate
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-key\fR \fIPATH\fR
a next key, from init or the last rotate; give one \-\-next\-key per key when several must sign; '\-' means standard input
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the new next key, outside DIR; '\-' means standard output
.TP
\fB\-\-same\-next\fR
keep the next keys you have, instead of making new ones
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
where to write the new current key (default: in place of DIR/current.key)
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-pub\fR \fIPATH\fR
add another person's public key (from \fBwrit pubkey\fR) to the current keys
.TP
\fB\-\-key\-k\fR \fIK\fR
how many current keys must sign each statement (default: more than half of them)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-n\fR \fIN\fR
how many new next keys to make (default: 1)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-k\fR \fIK\fR
how many next keys must sign a rotation (default: more than half of them)
.TP
\fB\-\-next\-pub\fR \fIPATH\fR
add another person's public key (from \fBwrit pubkey\fR) to the next keys
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-validity\fR \fIDUR\fR
set a new limit on how long statements may last
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-max\-validity\fR
